# **Rule 42/43 proportionate reversal, where the period declares exempt
# turnover** — Plan 123 Slice H.
#
# Where inputs serve both taxable and exempt or non-business use, credit is
# available only in the taxable proportion.
#
# **This is a period-level figure, not a per-invoice one**, and that is why a
# product built entirely from invoice-to-invoice rules cannot see it at all.
# The reversal is computed on the period's own turnover split, so the rule
# reports the *period* as its subject — the first rule in this pack to do so.
#
# What it does **not** do is compute the reversal. The formula needs exempt
# turnover, total turnover, common credit and a capital-goods life, and a rule
# that guessed any of them would put a number into a filed return that nobody
# derived. It reports that a reversal is **due** and names the figures the
# period declared; the amount is the preparer's, computed where the inputs are
# visible.

PREFIX gst: <https://graph-owl.dev/packs/gst#>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>

SELECT ?filingPeriod ?period ?exemptTurnover ?totalTurnover
WHERE {
  GRAPH ?periods {
    ?filingPeriod a gst:FilingPeriod ;
                  gst:period          ?period ;
                  gst:exemptTurnover  ?exemptTurnover ;
                  gst:totalTurnover   ?totalTurnover .
  }

  # `xsd:decimal(...)` rather than `> 0`: the register writes these as plain
  # string literals (`rows_to_turtle` writes every value as a string), and a
  # string comparison against 0 is either an error or lexicographic — either
  # way it does not mean "there was exempt turnover". Casting says what is
  # meant, and a value that will not cast simply does not match rather than
  # failing the whole query.
  FILTER (xsd:decimal(?exemptTurnover) > 0)
}
ORDER BY ?period
